report.built:
I built an automatic quality controller, engine/editeur/controle.py (new, about 1,870 lines, Python comments in French without accents, all user-facing text in French). You run it as `python -m editeur.controle <reel.json | job.json | project_dir --reel id> [--video file.mp4] [--debut s] [--pas 0.5] [--sortie dir] [--json]`. It can also be called from Python with `controle.controler(reel, video=None, debut=0, sortie=None)` and `controle.resume(report)`.

(a) JSON pass (no render, about 5–10 s for the 71 s reel)
- Timing comes from remotion/src/timeline.ts itself, through a small Node module written to a temp folder. It uses videoSegments, pages, designScenes, timedWords, framing, motivatedZoom and validCutout, so no timing code is duplicated.
- The positions and sizes of on-screen elements copy what Reel.tsx, design.tsx and graphics.tsx do. It covers visibility logic (extras, plannedGraphic, manual, designed, outro), captions, the editorial kinetic text, every scene type, every graphic, banner, emoji, logo, the Tech 3D studio header strip (studio title and running clock) and the VHS / documentary decor.
- Checks:
  - overlays that overlap each other;
  - anything on screen during the hook except captions and banner (the hook comes from the creativePlan hook section, otherwise from the first sentence);
  - text over the face: the face box comes from the analysis-v3 face data, with outlier rejection and a median over neighbouring samples; the eyes-to-mouth area is weighted;
  - text too small for the format (captions, main text and secondary text each have their own thresholds, normalized to 1080 px);
  - low contrast, with WCAG luminance and allowance for outline and shadow. The background is rebuilt from source frames at 1/4 resolution, 2 fps; in studio / collage modes the person is composited from the cached cutout alpha.
  - cards that blend into the background;
  - internal or debug text on screen: the reel title such as « Vidéo 1 », take / version names, the header strip's clock, EXEMPLE / ILLUSTRATION, file names, undefined / NaN, known Whisper additions. It applies the same filter as design.tsx, so hidden secondary text is ignored.
  - stuttered words and repeated messages;
  - empty top or bottom third on a flat background for long stretches;
  - no visual change for too long (cuts, visible zooms, element entrances; a slow push-in of at least 4 % counts as movement);
  - zooms that cannot be seen because the Tech 3D studio covers the video;
  - missing media: proxies, original rushes, voice, music, logo, graphics images, cutout frames and frame count (a cutout shorter than its shot freezes the person);
  - cutout engine: old MediaPipe engine, or the RVM qualite.json score and fallback ranges from the new detourage.py;
  - light mismatch between the person and the studio;
  - music level under the voice (ebur128 on the music file, with the 0.45 ducking factor, against the 18–25 dB norm);
  - text in the app safe zones.

(b) Video pass, optional (about 15 s for the 14 s preview)
- Resolution and duration.
- FFmpeg blackdetect and freezedetect.
- ebur128: target −14 LUFS ±1, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP. On short previews these findings are only advisory.
- Frames sampled every 0.5 s:
  - MediaPipe face detector (analyze.detect_face): face not found, or a text element over the detected face.
  - Text legibility measured on the frame (Otsu split between text and background), skipped during entrance and exit animations.
  - Card contrast against its surroundings.
  - Cutout audit on up to 14 frames: the cutout alpha actually used is compared with an RVM resnet50 reference matte of the source frame. It measures leftover background pieces as a % of the person (with a check that they are visible on the rendered frame), detached blobs, grey semi-transparent smears, edge transition width in on-screen px compared with the reference, and mask detail compared with the reference.
- It saves PNG evidence (side-by-side crops with the leftovers in red, element crops).
- GPU memory is released afterwards (torch.cuda.empty_cache).

Output
- The report is `{version, reel, video, note /10, verdict, compte, constats[], mesures, duree_controle_s}`.
- Each finding has `{gravite: 'bloquant'|'important'|'conseil', code, temps, fin, message, suggestion, preuve}`, plus optional `zone` [x0, y0, x1, y1] in px to draw on the player and `element` id.
- It writes controle.json and controle.txt, readable French text, and a preuves/ folder.
- Score: 10 minus penalties (bloquant 3, important 1, conseil 0.3). A repeated finding code counts half and has a cap. Any bloquant caps the score at 4.9.
- `resume()` gives a compact version to store as `reel.controle`.

Tests
- docs/qualite-2026-09-30/tests/test_controle.py
- docs/qualite-2026-09-30/tests/fabriquer_cas_propre.py builds the clean reel from a COPY.

